@pkozlowski-opensource [`class`] has the same issue, should be removed when exp is `null` or `undefined`.

``` typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'my-app',
  styles: ['.c {color: red}'],
  template: `
  <h1 [class]="v1">v1</h1>
  <h1 [class]="v2">v2</h1>
  <h1 [class]="v3">v3</h1>`
})
export class MyApp {
  v1: string = "c";
  v2: string = null;
  v3: string;
}

bootstrap(MyApp);
```

Generated HTML:

``` html
<h1 class="c">v1</h1>
<h1 class="null">v2</h1>
<h1 class="undefined">v3</h1>
```

@todoubaba this is sth entirely different. The comparable syntax would be `[class.foo]="null"` which AFAIK works correctly.

What you are raising here is binding to the `className` property, so equivalent of:

``` javascript
var myDiv = document.createElement('div');
myDiv.className = null;
```

which produces `<div class="null"></div>` (at least in Chrome). So this is not Angular really, but a browser doing type casting / conversion.

Feel free to open a separate issue for this, but it is disputable is we should be fixing this.
